The **F distribution** is a ratio of two independent chi-square variables, each divided by its degrees of freedom: $F = (\chi^2_{
u_1}/
u_1)/(\chi^2_{
u_2}/
u_2)$. Applied to two samples it becomes the ratio of sample variances $s_1^2/s_2^2$ - so F answers "is this strategy genuinely more volatile than that one, or is the difference noise?". Values near 1 are consistent with equal variances; extreme values signal a real difference in scale.
